<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marshall Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marshall Islands are two chains of coral atolls in the central Pacific, the Ratak and Ralik chains, home to Marshallese navigators for some two thousand years. They became a German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1885, were occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1914 and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1944. After serving as a US nuclear test site, they became independent in free association in 1986.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marshall-islands.jpg" alt="Marshall Islands" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Marshall_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>German traders dominated copra buying in the <b>Marshall Islands</b> from the 1870s, and in 1878 a German naval officer secured a treaty with chiefs on Jaluit.</blockquote><a href="/state/marshall-islands-protectorate">Marshall Islands Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in the First World War the Japanese navy seized Germany's island colonies north of the equator: the Marianas except Guam, the Carolines, Palau and the <b>Marshalls</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-seas-mandate">South Seas Mandate</a></li><li><blockquote>The districts chose separate paths: the Northern Mariana Islands became a US commonwealth, while the <b>Marshall Islands</b> and the Federated States of Micronesia gained independence in free association in 1986.</blockquote><a href="/state/trust-territory-of-the-pacific-islands">Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands</a></li></ul></section>
